 • In the collection phase, if the robot’s battery falls below 10%, it must abort the normal procedure and instead go to a recharging station and reassign the task. This introduces a fallback alternative.  
 • Similarly, in the delivery phase, if the battery falls below 30%, the robot must return the resource to a checkpoint and reassign the remaining delivery. Again, this is modeled as a fallback alternative.  
 • Therefore, both G2 and G3 are decomposed with a Fallback operator between a “normal” sub-goal and a “battery management” sub-goal.

3. Task Decomposition  
 • Under the normal collection sub-goal (G2.1), we define tasks for navigation, resource request, and waiting for retrieval.  
 • The battery management alternative (G2.2) is decomposed into tasks for navigating to the recharging station, recharging, and reassigning the mission.  
